ACTUAL THUMB WORD TRANSFERS TRANSLATED INTO ENGLISH.

A bottom-up English Major C translation of machine encodings from
a pinned external assembler expectation, preserving their original
instruction identity rather than constructing a fictitious program.

Source repository: Android Open Source Project, ART.
Pinned source revision: 08490b84048a0267694268185441b70cfa090185.
Pinned file:
compiler/utils/assembler_thumb_test_expected.cc.inc
https://android.googlesource.com/platform/art/+/08490b84048a0267694268185441b70cfa090185/compiler/utils/assembler_thumb_test_expected.cc.inc

The original source includes THREE DIFFERENT arrays:
  StoreMultipleResults,
  LoadMultipleResults,
  SpecialAddSubResults.

These are separate expected-disassembly fragments. The byte sequences
below come from separate source sections and were NOT observed as one
combined function in an Android program. For this translation, their
starting register and memory states are stated separately. No
source-level R, C, or C++ routine is being reconstructed.

Architecture: symbolic ARMv7-A processor executing the T32 instruction
set in unprivileged user mode. These four selected instructions all
occupy TWO BYTES. For physical execution, the memory mappings,
endianness, permissions and exact CPU are still unspecified.

PART ONE. THE FOUR ENCODINGS ACTUALLY RECORDED IN THE SOURCE.

The AOSP expected assembly at StoreMultipleResults offset zero:
    0xC409  :  STMIA R4!, {R0,R3}

The expected assembly at LoadMultipleResults offset zero:
    0xCC09  :  LDMIA R4!, {R0,R3}

The expected assembly in SpecialAddSubResults:
    offset 0x0002  0xB014  : ADD SP, #80
    offset 0x0014  0xB094  : SUB SP, #80

Their short assembly spellings are recorded as provenance.
The actual executable-English operation names are the
long names assigned in the initial grant:

    operation 57 for this STMIA;
    operation 58 for this LDMIA;
    operation 59 for this stack-pointer addition;
    operation 60 for this stack-pointer subtraction.

The encoding 0xC409 in binary is 1100010000001001.
Its first five bits, 11000, select the T1 STMIA class.
Its next three bits, 100, select R4 as base.
Its last eight bits, 00001001, select bit zero
and bit three of the low-register mask, meaning
R0 and R3. The number of transferred words is TWO.

The encoding 0xCC09 in binary is 1100110000001001.
Its first five bits, 11001, select the T1 LDMIA class.
Its next three bits select R4, and its eight-bit
mask again selects R0 and R3.

Both 0xB014 and 0xB094 contain the low seven-bit
field 0b0010100, unsigned value 20. The
four-byte scaling of that immediate makes the
operational displacement 80 bytes.
The distinguishable subtract-selecting bit
in 0xB094 changes the direction of the arithmetic;
it does NOT change the size of the displacement.

PART TWO. THE STORE-MULTIPLE SPECIMEN.

Define the initial execution index to be OFFSET ZERO
within StoreMultipleResults, NOT an actual process VA.

Let the following machine state be explicitly given:
    R4 = 0x00003000.
    R0 = 0x11223344.
    R3 = 0x55667788.
    N, Z, C, V are unspecified but fixed.
    The four-byte words at 0x3000 and 0x3004
    belong to a mapped writable aligned memory region.

At index zero, the 16-bit word 0xC409 is decoded as
the permitted operation whose full name starts:
"Store the original thirty-two-bit contents
of each selected low general-purpose register
to a consecutive aligned memory word ...".

The instruction's writeback base register is R4.
The selected register set is {R0,R3}.
Its smallest member is R0.
Its next member is R3.
The base register R4 is NOT itself in the list.

At address 0x3000 store the four-byte representation
of the original value of R0, namely 0x11223344.

At address 0x3004 store the four-byte representation
of the original value of R3, namely 0x55667788.

After both stores complete normally, replace R4
with 0x3000 + 2*4 = 0x3008.

Do not modify R0, R3, SP, LR or N/Z/C/V.
There is no branch and the successor instruction
would begin at offset 0x0002 within this source
fragment, if such instruction bytes were supplied.
No later instruction's effects are asserted here.

For an ordinary little-endian DATA memory representation,
the actual four memory bytes at 0x3000..0x3003
would be 0x44,0x33,0x22,0x11.
The bytes at 0x3004..0x3007 would be
0x88,0x77,0x66,0x55.
This endian-specific expansion is a model choice,
not an observation of the example on actual hardware.

PART THREE. THE LOAD-MULTIPLE SPECIMEN.

Use a DIFFERENT starting state and different source array.

Let the index be OFFSET ZERO in LoadMultipleResults.
Assume R4=0x3000 and two valid readable aligned
memory words:
    memory[0x3000..0x3003] = 0x11223344;
    memory[0x3004..0x3007] = 0x55667788.
Let prior R0 and R3 be unspecified.
Let N, Z, C, V be unknown but fixed.

The halfword 0xCC09 maps to operation 58,
whose full name begins:
"Read consecutive aligned thirty-two-bit
memory words beginning at the address
in a separate low general-purpose base register ...".

From the word at 0x3000 replace R0 with 0x11223344.
From the word at 0x3004 replace R3 with 0x55667788.
When both reads complete normally, advance R4
by eight bytes, making R4=0x3008.

Do not modify the condition flags. Do not
change SP, LR, or the execution state.
The successor index is offset 0x0002 in the
LoadMultipleResults instruction stream.

The absolute target machine addresses in
both specimens are expressly chosen inputs;
the AOSP source only pins the halfwords and
registers named in the expected disassembly.

PART FOUR. STACK POINTER ARITHMETIC WITHOUT MEMORY EFFECTS.

Again use a SEPARATE starting machine state.
Let the initial SP equal 0x00002000.

At offset 0x0002 of SpecialAddSubResults,
0xB014 performs the permitted stack-pointer
addition. Its immediate field is 20 words,
equivalent to eighty bytes. If executed with
the given state, SP becomes 0x00002050.
Memory is not read or written, and the flags
N/Z/C/V do not change. Sequential index
within the fragment would become 0x0004.

At offset 0x0014 of SpecialAddSubResults,
0xB094 performs the permitted stack-pointer
subtraction. If executed FROM THE ORIGINAL
SP=0x2000 rather than from the prior
hypothetical ADD result, its result
would be SP=0x00001FB0.

A THIRD HYPOTHETICAL two-instruction sequence
could execute 0xB014 followed by 0xB094
with no intervening stack change, giving
SP=0x2000 again. That sequence is not
the original assembler fixture's adjacent
instruction stream. Label it a synthetic
semantic composition, not source disassembly.

PART FIVE. WHAT IS NOT DERIVED FROM THE ENCODINGS.

None of these four halfwords performs a function call,
allocates DRAM, maps virtual memory, changes process
privilege, or produces a floating-point number.

The stored words are data. Executing the instruction
0xC409 does NOT make either stored word executable
ARM machine code. Loading data from 0x3000 does
not transfer control there.

The actual AOSP fixture includes more instruction
forms not translated IN THIS PARTICULAR DOCUMENT,
including 32-bit load/store multiple encodings with
high registers. The fact that those forms are next
in the source array does not permit 57/58 to
silently broaden into them. A separate document,
"Actual wide Thumb multiple-word operations in long
English.english", now translates four of those forms
using operations 61 through 64, with distinct
writeback semantics. Neither document grants
the full ARMv7-A T32 instruction set.

Do not claim that the four samples were executed
on a real processor, combined into a compiled
function, or translated with a working
English Major C implementation.

PART SIX. REQUESTED POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE TESTS.

Positive test: decode first five bits of 0xC409
to T1 store-multiple, base to R4, mask to {R0,R3},
write back base by eight bytes.

Positive test: decode 0xCC09 to T1 load-multiple
with the same base and set, and load values in
ascending register order from ascending addresses.

Positive test: 0xB014 and 0xB094 must correspond
to opposite stack-pointer arithmetic with equal
magnitude eighty bytes and no data-memory access.

Negative test: swap R0 and R3 address assignments.
The test must reject this because register
list text order does not determine transfer order.

Negative test: count two selected registers
as sixteen bytes rather than eight; reject.

Negative test: interpret 0xB014's immediate as
twenty bytes; reject, because the encoding
scales it by four.

Negative test: infer a store to address 0x2050
from ADD SP,#80; reject, because the operation
only changes the register.

Negative test: treat the R4 writeback from LDMIA
as a write to the memory word at address 0x3008;
reject, because writeback changes the BASE REGISTER.

Negative test: claim that the AOSP fragments
show an actual consecutive program executing
all four operations; reject, because the
original records are independent arrays.

This text records the tests to be implemented.
It does not report a compiler, emulator, device,
or semantic-language implementation pass.
